117
00:12:02,320 --> 00:12:06,320
And I think that is important to discuss and to find out about.
我认为讨论并探究这一点很重要。

118
00:12:06,320 --> 00:12:16,320
Professor Piraz, maybe there are people watching us in Africa or Latin America, North America, for instance, Oceania.
皮拉斯教授，也许有来自非洲、拉丁美洲、北美洲，比如大洋洲的人正在观看我们。

119
00:12:16,320 --> 00:12:19,320
What about them when we talk about classics?
当我们谈论经典时，他们呢？

120
00:12:19,320 --> 00:12:27,320
Do they also have a place in the big discussion or is it just Chinese, Greek, Roman, Italian?
他们是否也有资格参与这场宏大讨论，还是说只有中国、希腊、罗马和意大利？

121
00:12:27,320 --> 00:12:35,320
That's a good question because the point is that our very old civilization, in the sense that civilization,
这是个很好的问题，因为关键在于，就文明而言，我们古老的文明

122
00:12:35,320 --> 00:12:45,320
they reflected about themselves since hundreds and hundreds of years have a clear conception of the past.
在数百年的自我反思中，对过去有着清晰的概念。

123
00:12:45,320 --> 00:12:55,320
But you have to consider that the ancient, and I mean the ancient Greek and Romans, had a lot of confrontation with other peoples.
但你们必须考虑到，古代，我是说古希腊人和罗马人，与其他民族有很多接触。

124
00:12:55,320 --> 00:13:04,320
They were open to confrontation, at least in Mediterranean, because obviously of the limitation of technology, of traveling and so on.
至少在 Mediterranean 地区，他们对接触持开放态度，因为显然受限于旅行等技术条件。

125
00:13:04,320 --> 00:13:08,320
But they were absolutely open to other civilized areas.
但他们对其他文明地区绝对持开放态度。

126
00:13:08,320 --> 00:13:15,320
Romans in particular, as they increased, they enlarged their empire.
特别是罗马人，随着他们的扩张，他们扩大了帝国。

127
00:13:15,320 --> 00:13:27,320
They confronted themselves with the new peoples and they did not impose their culture and their customs on the others.
他们面对新的民族，并没有将自己的文化和习俗强加于人。

128
00:13:27,320 --> 00:13:32,320
They absorbed them, but they not impose their culture.
他们吸收了这些文化，而不是强加自己的文化。

129
00:13:32,320 --> 00:13:40,320
That is a very important example for us and for the confrontation with other civilizations.
这对我们以及与其他文明的接触来说，是一个非常重要的例子。

130
00:13:40,320 --> 00:13:46,320
They don't know ancient Greek and Roman civilizations.
他们不了解古希腊和罗马文明。

131
00:13:46,320 --> 00:13:54,320
That is the point. We have to be very open to confrontation, to other cultures.
这就是重点。我们必须对接触、对其他文化保持非常开放的态度。

132
00:13:54,320 --> 00:14:00,320
What do you mean confrontation? What do you mean they were open to confrontation with other cultures?
你所说的“接触”是什么意思？你说他们对其他文化的接触持开放态度，这是什么意思？

133
00:14:00,320 --> 00:14:05,320
Were you saying that they were eager and willing to learn to interact with other cultures?
你是说他们渴望并愿意学习如何与其他文化互动吗？

134
00:14:05,320 --> 00:14:12,320
Or they were very, how should I say, they were very adventurous and they wanted to conquer other places?
还是说他们非常，该怎么说呢，他们非常爱冒险，想要征服其他地方？

135
00:14:12,320 --> 00:14:14,320
Absolutely not.
绝对不是。

136
00:14:14,320 --> 00:14:28,320
Because that's the impression a lot of people would have, that western cultures had a tendency to go out, to reach out, to go to new places and expand.
因为很多人会有这样的印象，认为西方文化倾向于走出去，去探索，去新的地方扩张。

137
00:14:28,320 --> 00:14:34,320
That is almost with a connotation of inviting confrontation or starting confrontation.
这几乎带有一种挑衅或引发对抗的含义。

138
00:14:34,320 --> 00:14:42,320
You are right. I think that is a very old tendency of the past centuries of Europe and western civilization.
你说得对。我认为这是过去几个世纪欧洲和西方文明的一种非常古老的倾向。

139
00:14:42,320 --> 00:14:52,320
I think now that the modern European culture is not so eager of conquering other cultures.
我认为现在的欧洲文化并不那么热衷于征服其他文化。

140
00:14:52,320 --> 00:14:56,320
I think it's the contrary. It's absolutely the contrary.
我认为恰恰相反。绝对是相反的。

141
00:14:56,320 --> 00:15:03,320
We are eager to understand the others, the other culture and to confront with them.
我们渴望理解他人，理解其他文化，并与之交流。

142
00:15:03,320 --> 00:15:07,320
That is a very common widespread sense.
这是一种非常普遍的看法。

143
00:15:07,320 --> 00:15:12,320
I think also in ordinary people, not only in academics.
我认为这在普通人中也是如此，而不仅仅是在学术界。

144
00:15:13,320 --> 00:15:18,320
That would be great if that is the case.
如果情况确实如此，那就太好了。

145
00:15:18,320 --> 00:15:22,320
I believe you also have a good point there.
我相信你在那里也有一个很好的观点。

146
00:15:22,320 --> 00:15:25,320
I just wanted to be the devil's advocate.
我只是想唱唱反调。

147
00:15:25,320 --> 00:15:31,320
Professor Zhang, let's come to now and today when we talk about this topic.
张教授，让我们回到现在，当我们谈论这个话题时。

148
00:15:31,320 --> 00:15:35,320
For a lot of people this may sound very far-fetched.
对很多人来说，这听起来可能非常牵强。

149
00:15:35,320 --> 00:15:40,320
Either talking about far away lands or people or works or talk about ancient words.
要么谈论遥远的土地、人物或作品，要么谈论古代的文字。

150
00:15:40,320 --> 00:15:49,320
Why now? Why are we looking into classics for clues to solving today's problem?
为什么是现在？为什么我们要从经典中寻找解决当今问题的线索？

151
00:15:49,320 --> 00:15:52,320
Can we really find clues?
我们真的能找到线索吗？

152
00:15:52,320 --> 00:15:59,320
Well, I think as the society becomes more and more industrialized,
嗯，我认为随着社会日益工业化，

153
00:15:59,320 --> 00:16:06,320
actually we need more ancient wisdom because we have encountered a lot of problems.
我们实际上更需要古老的智慧，因为我们遇到了许多问题。

154
00:16:06,320 --> 00:16:15,320
For example, the division labor has become more and more advanced.
例如，分工变得越来越精细。

155
00:16:15,320 --> 00:16:18,320
But then this will cut off.
但这会切断……

156
00:16:18,320 --> 00:16:22,320
This will cut your knowledge, the picture of knowledge into pieces.
这会将你的知识、知识的整体图景割裂成碎片。

157
00:16:22,320 --> 00:16:28,320
Everyone is incapable of one profession but without knowing the knowledge of others.
每个人只精通一门职业，却对其他领域的知识一无所知。

158
00:16:28,320 --> 00:16:34,320
This will bring a challenge to your own development of your personality.
这将对你自身人格的发展带来挑战。

159
00:16:34,320 --> 00:16:43,320
Then classics will tell us that you always look at how to develop personality fully, comprehensively.
而经典著作会告诉我们，要始终关注如何全面、完整地发展人格。

160
00:16:43,320 --> 00:16:51,320
For example, now we have the challenge of technologies like AI, AI is shaping our society dramatically.
例如，现在我们面临着人工智能等技术的挑战，AI正在深刻地重塑我们的社会。

161
00:16:51,320 --> 00:16:55,320
How could we manage development of AI?
我们该如何管理AI的发展？

162
00:16:55,320 --> 00:17:03,320
Then we have to go back to the wisdom of classics to see whether those arts has ethical purpose.
那么我们就必须回归经典的智慧，看看这些技术是否具有伦理目的。

163
00:17:03,320 --> 00:17:10,320
We always use these ethical targets to manage those technologies.
我们总是利用这些伦理目标来管理那些技术。

164
00:17:10,320 --> 00:17:15,320
Also, in terms of questions like peace and war and political order,
此外，在和平与战争、政治秩序等问题上，

165
00:17:15,320 --> 00:17:21,320
I think there is always the concern about whether there is material expansion.
我认为人们始终关注是否存在物质扩张的问题。

166
00:17:21,320 --> 00:17:29,320
Those expansion of glory, honor, discussion of all these things can truly lead to human happiness.
这些关于荣耀、荣誉以及所有这些事物的探讨，确实能带来人类的幸福。

167
00:17:29,319 --> 00:17:36,319
So I think the classical wisdom always asks what is the real purpose of human life?
所以我认为，古典智慧总是追问人生的真正目的是什么？

168
00:17:36,319 --> 00:17:44,319
Whether there is today's like militarism, those discussions about the political civilization,
无论是当今的军国主义，还是那些关于政治文明的讨论，

169
00:17:44,319 --> 00:17:47,319
those kinds of things really can bring us happiness.
这类事情真的能给我们带来幸福吗？

170
00:17:47,319 --> 00:17:49,319
I think all these are classics.
我认为这些都属于经典。

171
00:17:49,319 --> 00:17:51,319
What classic can bring us to us?
经典能带给我们什么？

172
00:17:51,319 --> 00:17:55,319
Professor Zhang, I'm going to go very straight to you for one tip.
张教授，我想直接向您请教一个建议。

173
00:17:55,319 --> 00:18:04,319
Can you share a tip to our viewers as to one piece of advice, one piece of work you can quote?
您能否给观众分享一条建议，或者引用一部作品？

174
00:18:04,319 --> 00:18:05,819
You can think of at this moment.
您此刻能想到的。

175
00:18:05,319 --> 00:18:07,319
That will help people answer these questions.
这将有助于人们回答这些问题。

176
00:18:07,319 --> 00:18:16,319
Well, for example, as you know, the Chinese sage Lao Zi has a saying,
嗯，例如，正如大家所知，中国圣人老子有一句名言，

177
00:18:16,319 --> 00:18:23,319
Dao Fa Zi Ran, which in the Dao is a great way, actually follows the nature.
“道法自然”，意思是道遵循的是自然规律。

178
00:18:23,319 --> 00:18:36,319
So actually, this is really kind of discussion about teaching about the relationship between our arts and the moral purpose of the words.
所以，这实际上是一种关于艺术与道德目的之间关系的探讨。

179
00:18:36,319 --> 00:18:46,319
And also, the Chinese sage always talk about the all under heaven, Tian Xia, Tian Xia Wei Gong,
此外，中国圣人总是谈论天下，天下为公，

180
00:18:46,319 --> 00:18:49,319
the whole under heaven for the public.
即天下是公众的。

181
00:18:49,319 --> 00:18:57,319
And these are very important teaching about how to avoid war and how to bring the solidarity of human being.
这些关于如何避免战争以及如何促进人类团结的教诲非常重要。

182
00:18:57,319 --> 00:19:03,319
Professor Hans Van Es, do you think these examples that Professor Zhang just raised,
汉斯·范·埃斯教授，您认为张教授刚才提到的这些例子，

183
00:19:03,319 --> 00:19:09,319
do you think they can be understood in the first place by people in other parts of the world outside of China?
您认为中国以外其他地区的人首先能理解它们吗？

184
00:19:09,319 --> 00:19:15,319
And secondly, maybe they provide a guidance, but can they really be actionable?
其次，也许它们提供了指导，但它们真的具有可操作性吗？

185
00:19:15,319 --> 00:19:20,319
Because it's very easy to say there is a broad philosophy, but when you put it in your action,
因为说有一套宏大的哲学很容易，但当你把它付诸行动时，

186
00:19:20,319 --> 00:19:25,319
even I as a Chinese person can find it a bit, where do I start?
甚至像我这样的中国人也会觉得有点……我该从哪里开始呢？

187
00:19:25,319 --> 00:19:31,319
Yeah. Well, I actually think these texts can be understood, but we,
是的。嗯，我实际上认为这些文本是可以被理解的，但我们，

188
00:19:31,319 --> 00:19:36,319
we, sinologists have to work hard in order to present them in a way that they can be understood,
我们汉学家必须努力工作，以便以人们能够理解的方式呈现它们，

189
00:19:36,319 --> 00:19:45,319
because the way how previous translators transported Chinese ideas do not really conform to the reading habits of today's public.
因为以往翻译者传达中国思想的方式并不真正符合当今公众的阅读习惯。

190
00:19:45,319 --> 00:19:50,319
And that is a problem. But I would like to add something else, which I think is important.
这是一个问题。但我想补充一点，我认为这很重要。

191
00:19:50,319 --> 00:19:57,319
I do think that classical studies is not so much about these eternal wisdoms that you can learn.
我认为古典研究并不主要是关于那些你可以学到的永恒智慧。

192
00:19:57,319 --> 00:20:03,319
They are important too, and you will find them from time to time when reading these classics.
它们也很重要，你在阅读这些经典时会时不时地发现它们。

193
00:20:03,319 --> 00:20:10,319
But the important thing about classics, the study of classics is that it trains you to do critical thinking.
但关于经典，研究经典的重要之处在于它训练你进行批判性思维。

194
00:20:10,319 --> 00:20:21,319
And that is the resource that human beings are most needing right now, I think, in the age of AI,
我认为，在人工智能时代，这是人类目前最需要的资源，

195
00:20:21,319 --> 00:20:27,319
when the internet can provide you easy solutions for any problem right now,
当互联网可以为你任何问题的提供现成解决方案时，

196
00:20:27,319 --> 00:20:38,319
it's important to know that to study a classic is something that trains your brain and that shows you that there are different solutions to many different things.
重要的是要知道，研读经典能锻炼你的大脑，并让你明白许多不同的事物都有多种不同的解决方案。

197
00:20:38,319 --> 00:20:47,319
I've been studying the Chinese classic Monser for the last six or so months, once again, Monser in Latin.
过去六个月左右，我一直在研读中国经典《蒙瑟》，再次强调，是用拉丁文研读的《蒙瑟》。

198
00:20:47,319 --> 00:20:54,319
And I always consider this text in prior times to be an easy text.
我一直认为这部文本在以前看来是一篇容易理解的文章。

199
00:20:54,319 --> 00:21:01,319
Now that I have been studying it closely, I found out there is not one single sentence in it, which is easy.
现在经过仔细研读，我发现其中没有一句话是容易的。

200
00:21:01,319 --> 00:21:10,319
It's difficult to understand these eternal wisdoms often turn out to be something that is not so easy to understand.
理解这些永恒的智慧往往发现它们并不那么容易理解。

201
00:21:10,319 --> 00:21:15,319
There are some sentences that seem to be quite clear, but on a second thought,
有些句子看起来相当清晰，但仔细一想，

202
00:21:15,319 --> 00:21:21,319
you realize they are actually very difficult and there's a second meaning behind it.
你会发现它们实际上非常难懂，背后还有第二层含义。

203
00:21:21,319 --> 00:21:23,319
And that is something you have to find out.
而这一点是你必须去发掘的。

204
00:21:23,319 --> 00:21:30,319
And I think this is something with all the experience that I now have with AI, AI does not tell you,
我认为，凭借我现在对人工智能的所有经验，人工智能不会告诉你这一点，

205
00:21:30,319 --> 00:21:37,319
because AI usually gives you some conservative solutions, which are very good for first reading.
因为人工智能通常给出一些保守的解决方案，这些对于初读来说非常好。

206
00:21:37,319 --> 00:21:46,319
The second reading is the interesting one, and this is something that modern human beings should learn and should not forget.
第二遍阅读才是有趣的，这是现代人应该学习且不应忘记的。

207
00:21:46,319 --> 00:21:51,319
That is my message to the world on the reading of classics.
这就是我对世界关于阅读经典的信息。

208
00:21:51,319 --> 00:22:00,319
Fascinating. Professor Piraz, once again, we talk a lot about Chinese civilization or other non-Western civilization,
令人着迷。皮拉斯教授，再次，我们谈论了很多关于中国文明或其他非西方文明，

209
00:22:00,319 --> 00:22:09,319
but should people also go back to the Greek and Roman classics and look for inspirations to solving their today's problems?
但人们是否也应该回到希腊和罗马经典中，寻找解决当今问题的灵感？

210
00:22:09,319 --> 00:22:17,319
Yeah, the point is that as the colleagues have already said, there is a lot of example of wisdom,
是的，关键在于，正如同事们已经说过的，有很多智慧的例子，

211
00:22:17,319 --> 00:22:28,319
of critical thinking about the world, about the human being, that ancient classics left for us.
关于世界、关于人类，以及古代经典留给我们的批判性思维。

212
00:22:28,319 --> 00:22:37,319
They gave us a lot of examples of these reflections, philosophers, but also thinkers, but also writers.
哲学家、思想家以及作家都为我们提供了许多这类反思的范例。

213
00:22:37,319 --> 00:22:46,319
A lot of Greek and Roman writers talked about human beings, critical points, critical issues of human beings.
许多希腊和罗马作家都探讨过人类，探讨人类的批判性观点和关键问题。

214
00:22:46,319 --> 00:22:55,319
So that is very important, I think, for modern people, for contemporary people, to read ancient authors
我认为，对于现代人和当代人来说，阅读古代作家的作品非常重要，

215
00:22:55,319 --> 00:23:01,319
thinking and writing about the same problems that modern people have.
因为他们思考和书写的问题正是现代人面临的问题。

216
00:23:01,319 --> 00:23:13,319
So it's important that we can think about this distance, chronological distance, it's very important because it learns to us to think historically,
因此，思考这种时间上的距离非常重要，因为它教会我们要以历史的眼光去思考，

217
00:23:13,319 --> 00:23:18,319
and so to increase our critical thinking.
从而增强我们的批判性思维。

218
00:23:18,319 --> 00:23:25,319
Well, these are very big topics and we have very limited time. So again, very actionable tips.
嗯，这些都是很大的话题，而我们的时间非常有限。所以，再次强调，给出一些非常实用的建议。

219
00:23:25,319 --> 00:23:32,319
If someone is interested in start re-engaging with classics, both in Chinese or in other languages,
如果有人有兴趣重新开始接触经典，无论是中文经典还是其他语言的经典，

220
00:23:32,319 --> 00:23:39,319
Professor Zhang and then Professor Hans Van Es and Professor Piraz, each one quick tip.
张教授，还有汉斯·范·埃斯教授和皮拉斯教授，请每人给出一条简短的建议。

221
00:23:39,319 --> 00:23:43,319
Where should they start and what they should not do?
他们应该从哪里开始，以及不应该做什么？

222
00:23:43,319 --> 00:23:52,319
I think our experience of studying Chinese classics is actually we start from poetry.
我认为，我们学习中国经典的经验是，实际上是从诗歌开始的。

223
00:23:52,319 --> 00:24:01,319
So like the ancient Chinese, the Shijing, when we are very small children, so actually we started with poetry,
就像中国古代的《诗经》，当我们还是小孩子的时候，实际上就是从诗歌开始的，

224
00:24:01,319 --> 00:24:08,319
we learned what is beautiful and then from what is beautiful and then we began to think about what is right, what is just,
我们学会了什么是美，然后从美出发，开始思考什么是对的，什么是正义的，

225
00:24:08,319 --> 00:24:14,319
and then we were reading other Confucian classics.
然后我们开始阅读其他儒家经典。

226
00:24:14,319 --> 00:24:17,319
What should they not do?
他们不应该做什么？

227
00:24:17,319 --> 00:24:25,319
Well, what they should not do is that simply in today's world, because you have AI,
嗯，他们不应该做的是，在当今这个世界，既然有了人工智能，

228
00:24:25,319 --> 00:24:32,319
then you simply ask the AI what kind of ideas are in these texts, then AI simply give you a very simple answer.
那就直接问人工智能这些文本里有什么观点，然后人工智能只会给你一个非常简单的答案。

229
00:24:32,319 --> 00:24:47,319
Just as the professor says, those answers tend to be very conservative, tend to be very simple, and it really lets you...
正如教授所说，那些答案往往非常保守，非常简略，这真的让你……

230
00:24:47,319 --> 00:24:53,319
Professor Hans Van Es, please don't repeat the answer about the AI.
汉斯·范·埃斯教授，请不要再重复关于人工智能的回答了。

231
00:24:53,319 --> 00:25:07,319
No, I think that, I mean, I'm talking as a sinologist who wants to make the Western reader know Chinese important texts,
不，我的意思是，我作为一名汉学家，希望让西方读者了解中国的重要文本，

232
00:25:07,319 --> 00:25:16,319
and so I think what people should start with are the Sushu, the four books that were put together by Juxi in the 12th century.
所以我认为人们应该从《四书》开始，那是朱熹在12世纪编纂的四部著作。

233
00:25:16,319 --> 00:25:20,319
They are already quite advanced, they are already quite advanced.
它们已经相当深奥了，确实相当深奥。

234
00:25:20,319 --> 00:25:29,319
They are quite advanced, but I guess if there are good translations of them, then start by reading one of them.
它们确实深奥，但我猜如果有好的译本，那就从其中一本开始读起。

235
00:25:29,319 --> 00:25:37,319
Don't read too much, it's too difficult to do this, so maybe it's better to just start with the lunyu, the Confucian analytics,
不要读太多，这太难了，所以也许最好先从《论语》开始，

236
00:25:37,319 --> 00:25:49,319
and try to understand what these chapters try to tell people, because this is what East Asian readers have been trained with for a couple of hundreds of years,
试着理解这些章节想告诉人们什么，因为东亚读者已经通过这种方式接受训练几百年了，

237
00:25:49,319 --> 00:25:52,319
and I think we should at least get this.
我认为我们至少应该掌握这一点。

238
00:25:52,319 --> 00:25:57,319
A second book then should be one Chinese novel.
第二本书应该是一部中国小说。

239
00:25:57,319 --> 00:26:03,319
They tend to be very long and that is difficult for Western readers to understand, the Shui-Ho-Juan, the...
它们往往非常长，这对西方读者来说很难理解，《水浒传》，那个……

240
00:26:03,319 --> 00:26:05,319
Outlaws of the Mosh.
《水浒传》。

241
00:26:05,319 --> 00:26:15,319
Yes, exactly, because this is something in a novel in which there are many day-to-day things that Chinese would immediately understand,
是的，完全正确，因为这是一部小说，里面有很多中国人一看就懂的日常生活细节，

242
00:26:15,319 --> 00:26:17,319
and Western readers don't know that.
而西方读者对此并不了解。

243
00:26:17,319 --> 00:26:19,319
What should they not do?
他们不应该做什么？

244
00:26:19,319 --> 00:26:27,319
I think they should not read what the Jesuits called, or what James Legg called the Chinese classics.
我认为他们不应该阅读耶稣会士，或者詹姆斯·莱格所称的中国经典。

245
00:26:27,319 --> 00:26:31,319
These are the Wu-Jing, the five canonical scriptures.
这些就是《五经》，即五部儒家经典。

246
00:26:31,319 --> 00:26:37,319
They are way too difficult and one will not so well understand what China is all about.
它们太难了，人们无法很好地理解中国究竟是怎么回事。

247
00:26:37,319 --> 00:26:42,319
This is something for advanced readers and it comes in a second step.
这是给进阶读者看的，属于第二步。

248
00:26:42,319 --> 00:26:44,319
Good, one step at a time.
很好，一步一步来。

249
00:26:44,319 --> 00:26:46,319
Finally, Professor Piraz.
最后，皮拉斯教授。

250
00:26:47,319 --> 00:26:57,319
I would suggest to read one of Greek or Latin classics, or Eastern Homer, or Roman poets like Virgil Horace,
我建议阅读一部希腊语或拉丁语经典，或者东方的荷马史诗，或者像维吉尔、贺拉斯这样的罗马诗人，

251
00:26:57,319 --> 00:27:01,319
Catullus, that would be a very pleasant to read.
卡图卢斯，那会是非常愉快的阅读体验。

252
00:27:01,319 --> 00:27:12,319
But I suggest to read that, and this is your second question, to read that on book and not on PC,
但我建议阅读这些，这也是你的第二个问题，要读纸质书而不是在电脑上读，

253
00:27:12,319 --> 00:27:16,319
because you have to think about, to reflect about that.
因为你需要思考，需要对此进行反思。

254
00:27:16,319 --> 00:27:22,319
So you have to take your time, and so I suggest to take it, to buy it, or to loan a book,
所以你必须花时间，因此我建议你去买一本，或者借一本书，

255
00:27:22,319 --> 00:27:29,319
and to read them in translation, obviously, but directly in books and not by PC.
并阅读它们的译本，显然，但直接通过纸质书而不是电脑。

256
00:27:29,319 --> 00:27:32,319
Classics in a classical way.
以古典的方式解读经典。

257
00:27:32,319 --> 00:27:34,319
Yeah, right, you're right.
是的，没错，你说得对。

258
00:27:34,319 --> 00:27:37,319
Thank you so much, we have to leave it there.
非常感谢，我们只能聊到这里了。

259
00:27:37,319 --> 00:27:40,319
I personally am fascinated by this topic, honestly.
说实话，我个人对这个话题非常着迷。

260
00:27:40,319 --> 00:27:42,319
But that's all the time we have for today.
但今天的时间就到这里了。

261
